+# NVFP4 MoE GEMV redesign notes (branch nv-free)
+
+Micro-benchmark: GGML_CUDA_INK_NVBENCH=<reps> GGML_CUDA_INK_NVBENCH_V=<variants> (see inkling-gemv.cu), run through
+/lab/scratch-nvfree/mb.sh (host wrapper scratch-nvfree/mbfmt.sh). us per launch incl. launch, 8 slots, real expert
+weights, rotating expert ids. gate_up = 23.89 MB (nb=36), down = 11.94 MB (nb=18). Very repeatable (+-0.05 us).
+
+## Ceilings (stream only, xor of loaded dwords)
+- empty kernel on the v1 grid: 1.96 us
+- gate_up: best stream 21.3 us (1.12 TB/s incl. launch): v3 load pattern L=18 PRE or L=36; aligned 16-byte L=32: 21.9;
+  v1 load pattern (L=8, 36-byte blocks, no PRE): 24.9-25.5
+- down: best stream 12.0 us (1.0 TB/s): L=18; L=8 PRE 12.8; L=8 no PRE 13.9
+- stream gets faster with more lanes per row / fewer sequential load rounds per wave / loads issued up front (PRE)
+
+## v1 (39de315): gate_up 30.5-30.7 us, down 18.7-18.8 us. 249 instructions per 64-value block iteration.
+ALU model: ~0.19 M wave-instructions/us for the whole GPU (ALU-only run of v3 L=8: 13.7 us), i.e. ALU is about half of
+the kernel and only partly hidden behind memory.
+
+## v3
+- signed e2m1 decode = xor of two v_perm_b32 (uses the special selectors 8..15), LUT for ue4m3, 3 loads per block,
+  y staged with 16-byte copies, y at LDS offset 0: ~170 instr per block iteration
+- L=8 (same summation order as v1): md5 b2b38cf906c9 == v1 (default config), with and without PRE3
+- micro-bench us (gate_up / down):
+  v1 30.53 / 18.68 ; v3 L8 27.32 / 16.31 ; v3 L8 PRE 25.92 / 15.74 ; L9 PRE 25.45 / 14.86 ; L12 PRE 23.83 / 15.17 ;
+  L16 PRE 24.94 / 16.09 ; L18 PRE 23.59 / 14.88 ; L6 PRE 27.40 / 15.47 ; L36 33.1 (per-wave setup dominates)
+- ALU only (MODE 2): L8 13.70/9.38, L9 13.14/8.27, L12 14.04/10.57, L18 14.85/9.88 ; setup only: L8 3.4, L18 5.3
+- lost: v2 of the previous attempt (y/-y in LDS + v_bfi select): L8 40.9 / 23.0, L4 31.6 / 18.2 -> dropped
+
+## Summation order
+- one partial sum per lane with L=18/18 (different fp order): tg128 87.0-87.6 vs v1 83.0, but kld FATTN=0: mean 0.001399,
+  same top p 99.608 -> FAILS acceptance (default config: 0.000994 / 99.739). Not usable.
+- chain mode (MODE 4): products kept per sub-block, the 8 v1 chains hop between lanes with ds_bpermute, then the v1 tree.
+  Bit-identical for any L >= 8: md5 b2b38cf906c9 for L 18/9, 12/18, 16/12 (same as v1 and v3 L8).
+  cost: gate_up L18 24.45 (plain 23.62), L12 24.57 ; down L9 15.25 (plain 14.92), L18 15.47
+- llama-bench tg128 (interleaved): v1 83.10/83.00 ; v3 L8 PRE 84.75/84.83 ; plain L18/18 86.97/87.59 ; plain 18/9 86.70/86.55
+- tg128 chain defaults (v3 18/9 CH3): 86.53/87.19 vs v1 83.67/83.96 ; L3DN=18: 87.17/87.48 ; L8/8: 84.58/85.37
+
+## Timing model (from MODE 2/3 probes)
+T ~= T_mem(load pattern) + serial instruction latency of one wave + ~1 us; total ALU is about half hidden.
+=> short waves (1 block per lane), all 64 lanes busy, little per-wave setup.
+
+## v4: products to LDS (phase 1), v1-order chains + tree in phase 2 (8 lanes per row)
+- thread t: block t % NB of row t / NB (+ R1*it), NIT blocks per thread; rows per 256-thread block = NIT*(256/NB)
+- micro-bench us (gate_up / down): NIT1 22.26 / 13.60 ; NIT2 23.51 / 14.86 ; NIT3 24.46 ; NIT4 26.20
+  stream of the same pattern: NIT1 20.70 / 12.10 ; NIT2 21.03 / 12.52
+- md5 b2b38cf906c9 (bit-identical to v1) for NIT 1/1 and 2/2
+- tg128 interleaved: v1 83.41/83.33 ; v3 chain 86.57/86.23 ; v4 1/1 90.16/88.46 ; v4 2/1 87.66/88.51 (noisy run)
+- v4 trims: 32-bit byte offsets (fewer scalar/vector multiplies). Things that LOST:
+  * ue4m3 table read from a global device table instead of computed per thread: 23.54 vs 22.76 (one more queued
+    memory round trip on the serial path of every wave costs far more than 12 ALU instructions)
+  * unconditional (branch-free) weight loads: 22.75 vs 22.42 conditional; the scheduler then emits the 3 loads of a
+    block in descending address order (32, 16, 0) instead of (32, 0, 16)
+  * volatile loads to force ascending order: 34 us (a wait after every load)
+- v4 final micro-bench: gate_up 22.28 us (1.07 TB/s incl. launch; stream of the same pattern 20.6-20.9),
+  down 13.36 us (0.89 TB/s; stream 12.0). v1: 30.53 / 18.57.
+- checks (final v4 default): kld default md5 b2b38cf906c9 (= v1), FATTN=0 md5 05c856b2ade6 (= v1 FATTN=0: mean KLD
+  0.000786, same top p 99.869), FATTN=0 NVFP4=0 BF16=0 md5 01ec923fac19
+
+## Final (commit after 3e6d82b)
+- llama-bench tg128, interleaved in one job, same binary: V=1 82.82/83.43/83.29/82.96 ; default (v4 NIT 1/1)
+  88.59/89.56/89.26/89.05. Earlier job: V=1 82.55/82.60/82.62, v4 89.46/89.37/88.30, v3 chain 86.29/86.53/88.06,
+  v4 NIT4DN=2 88.56/87.63/88.76.
+- pp512: V=1 659.30, default 657.83 (multi-token path untouched)
+- llama-completion greedy, 1425-token prompt + 320 tokens: identical output md5 for V=1, V=3, V=4, V=4 NIT 2/2
+- rocprofv3 (inflated): v4<36,1> 20.8 us/call (1.15 TB/s), v4<18,1> 13.5 us/call (0.88 TB/s); v1 23.3 us avg of both
+- left: ~1.5 us per launch between v4 and the stream probe of the same load pattern (20.6-20.9 / 12.0 us);
+  the stream probe itself is ~1.27 TB/s net of the 1.96 us empty launch.
+- not tried: issuing the 3 loads of a block in ascending order through inline asm, 512/1024-thread blocks,
+  float (f16 dot2) or MFMA paths (decode dominates: 104 of ~170 instructions per block are the nibble decode).
